{
  "dom_id": "step-q144845",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14214505/assets/69ae26109021b156ae22879f30a64c393fa9a870d207be72c75533c8b6918bb9.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[1,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $2$ and $5,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,6]$ given that $f(6)=3.$",
  "worked_solution": "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,2,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =3 \\\\ f(2) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{2}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-5 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ f(1) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{1}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-(5-2) \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{3,-2,0\\}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=6.$",
